  "strategy": "1. Approach and Rationale\nThis strategy employs an alternating projection method to minimize the maximum overlap in the Erd\u0151s problem. By iteratively projecting onto the constraint sets and updating based on the current maximum overlap, we aim to approach a solution adhering to both the integral and range constraints. The method combines gradient-based updates with feasibility repairs, focusing on the non-convex nature of the problem.\n\n2. Mathematical Specification\n- **Variables**: h \u2208 \u211d^n where n = n_points, each h[i] \u2208 [0, 1], and \u2211h[i] = n/2.\n- **Objective**: Minimize C\u2085 = max( np.correlate(h, 1 - h, mode=\"full\") * (2/n) ).\n- **Constraints**:\n  - 0 \u2264 h[i] \u2264 1 for all i.\n  - \u2211 h[i] = n / 2.\n- **Operations**:\n  - Gradient computation focuses on the current maximum overlap.\n  - Projection ensures constraint satisfaction after each update.\n\n3. Implementation Procedure\na. Initialization:\n   - If initial_h_values exists, validate and use as starting point. Otherwise, initialize h with a uniform distribution scaled to meet sum constraint.\n   - Ensure sum(h) = n/2 and each h[i] \u2208 [0, 1].\n\nb. Core Optimization Loop:\n   i. Compute overlaps using np.correlate.\n   ii. Identify lag k with maximum overlap.\n   iii. Compute gradient where each h[i] is updated based on its contribution to the overlap at k.\n   iv. Update h using a step size and apply gradient descent.\n   v. Project h into feasible region by clipping and scaling.\n\nc. Gradient Descent:\n   - Compute gradient as \u2207h = (1 - h_shifted) for identified k.\n   - Update h: h = h - step_size * gradient.\n   - Project h to [0,1] and scale to meet sum constraint.\n\nd. Termination:\n   - Stop after budget_s exhausted.\n   - Return best h and C\u2085.\n\n4. Search Choices and Variations\n- **Step Size**: Start with 0.01, adaptively reduce if no improvement.\n- **Projection Method**: Clipping followed by scaling.\n- **Lag Identification**: Focus on the dominant lag each iteration.\n\n5. Compute Budget Management\n- Convolution dominates computation; use FFT-based methods for efficiency.\n- Monitor time within loop to ensure termination before budget exhaustion.\n\n6. Validation and Return Contract\n- Ensure h satisfies constraints and compute C\u2085 using evaluator.\n- Final solution must be feasible with C\u2085 \u2264 0.380876.",
